== The Testimonies of Divines

These proofs from Scripture are enough to convince any Christian of the truth of the doctrine. Yet for your#super[+] further satisfaction I will briefly give you#super[+] the judgment of some ancient fathers, and of some modern divines, on the same.

#emph[Non qui habet et Servat, sed qui impertit est Dives, et impertitio, non autem possessio divites facit. Clemens Alexand.] Paedag. Lib. 3. Not he who possesses wealth and keeps it by him, but he who distributes it, is rich; nor is it the having but the laying out of riches that makes men rich.

#emph[Terrena omnia Servando amittimus, largiendo Servamus. Isiodorus.] We lose all earthly things by keeping them, and by giving them away we keep them.

#emph[Lucrum est egenis dare,] says St. Basil. It is the best way of thriving to give to those who are in want.

#emph[Faecundus est ager pauperum, cito reddit donantibus fructum. Aug. Serm. De Divite.] The field of the poor is very fruitful, and quickly yields an increase to the charitable.

The same Father, in his 25th sermon #emph[de verbis Domini] \[on the words of the Lord\], brings in Christ speaking like this: #emph[Da mihi ex eo quod dedi tibi, habuisti me largitorem, fac me debitorem,] etc. Give to me of what I have given to you. I have been your benefactor; make me your debtor. Become a usurer to me, and I will pay you with advantage. And in one of his epistles: #emph[Da modicum ut recipias centuplum.] Give a little, and you will receive a hundredfold.

#emph[Da pauperi ut detur tibi, quicquid pauperi dederis tu habebis, quod non dederis, habebit alter.] Give to the poor, and it will be given to you. Whatever you give to the poor, you secure to yourself; what you withhold, another will possess. #emph[Pet. Raven Serm. Quodam.]

#emph[Deus his solvit hoc faenus, scilicet semel in terra, Secundo in caelo. In terra a enim elecmosynariis multiplicat opes, in caelo autem longe plura, et major a rependit.] God pays twice what is lent to him: once in this world, by multiplying the wealth of almsgivers, and then in heaven, where he pays it over and over. #emph[Cornelius a lapid. In] Prov 19:17.

Dr. #emph[Hammond,] in his sermon on Deut 26:12, 13, lays down this proposition: that #emph[almsgiving or mercifulness] was never the #emph[wasting] or #emph[lessening] of any man’s estate, to #emph[himself] or his #emph[posterity,] but rather the #emph[increasing] of it. And on this he adds: If I have delivered a #emph[new doctrine,] which will not at once be believed, such as not every hearer will agree to, I do not doubt that there are plain texts of Scripture, more than one, which will assure every Christian of the truth of it. Consider them at your#super[+] leisure: Ps 41:1, 2; Ps 112, all to this purpose; Prov 11:25; 12:9; 19:17; 28:27. Add to these the words of Christ in Mark 10:30. Though they are spoken more #emph[generally] of any kind of parting with possessions for #emph[Christ’s sake,] St. #emph[Jerome] applies them to the words of #emph[Solomon] in Prov 11:24, “One scatters, yet increases all the more,” #emph[quia centuplum accipiunt in hoc tempore,] because, he says, they receive a hundredfold in this world.

All these put together, he says, must (to my understanding) make it as clear to any who acknowledge them as Scripture as if God should call to a man out of heaven by name, and bid him “relieve that poor man, and you will never be the poorer for it,” etc.

Afterward, in pressing the duty of almsgiving on his hearers, he says: This #emph[I am resolved on:] it is want of belief, and nothing else, that keeps men from the practice of this duty. If this one mountain could be removed, #emph[the lessening of our wealth,] of which almsgiving is accused; if that one stumbling block to flesh and blood could be kicked out of the way, there is no other devil that would take the unmerciful man’s part, and no other temptation that would trouble the almsgiver.

And let me tell you#super[+] this: you have no more evidence for #emph[the truth of Christ’s coming,] for #emph[all the fundamentals of your faith,] on which you are content that your salvation should depend, than such evidence as I have given you for your security in this point.

Dr. #emph[Jeremy Taylor,] in his #emph[Rules of Holy Living,] treating of alms (#emph[Sect.] 8), has this among other #emph[motives] to it: That portion of our estate out of which a tenth, or a fifth, or a twentieth, or some offering to God for religion and the poor goes forth, certainly returns with a great blessing on all the rest. It is like the pouring out of oil by the #emph[Sidonian woman:] as long as she poured into empty vessels, it could never stop running. Or it is like the #emph[widow’s barrel of meal:] it was not used up as long as she fed the prophet.

Mr. #emph[Hildersham,] in his 22nd lecture on Ps 51, speaking of #emph[almsgiving,] says: “It is a duty to which God has made greater promises than to almost any other that a Christian can perform.” And there are three sorts of promises made to it. First, “that it will never hinder or beggar a man; that what is given in this way will not be lost.” Yes, it will return again with advantage and increase; yes, what is given in this way will bring God’s blessing on all that we have besides (Deut 15:10).

Dr. #emph[Thomas Jacomb,] in his sermon on Matt 5:7, preached at the Spital in 1657, says: God will return to the merciful man what he gives to the poor. He will reward him #emph[in kind:] whatever it is that he bestows, it will be made up to him again #emph[in the very kind.] Many have found this to be true. In the morning they have given, it may be, five shillings, and before night God has unexpectedly brought them in twenty for it. #emph[The best way for a man to increase his estate is charity:] money here is like the widow’s oil; the more it is #emph[poured out,] the more it #emph[increases,] etc. And on p. 26: No man will be the poorer at the year’s end for what he lays out on the poor; you#super[+] will have your money again, and a profit on it too.

The author of #emph[The Whole Duty of Man,] treating of alms in Partition 17, says there is only one objection to be made against a cheerful giving of alms, and that is #emph[the danger of impoverishing oneself by what one gives.] To this he answers in these words: This is surely a vain supposition, since God has particularly promised the contrary to the charitable, #emph[that it will bring blessings on them, even in these outward things.] “The liberal soul will be made fat, and he who waters will himself be watered also” (Prov 11:25). “Whoever gives to the poor will not lack” (Prov 28:27). And there are many such texts, so that one may truly say this objection is grounded in direct unbelief. The short of it is, we dare not trust God. For this giving to the poor is directly putting our wealth into his hands: “Whoever is kind to the poor lends to the #smallcaps[Lord]” (Prov 19:17). And that, too, on a solemn promise of repayment, as it follows in that verse: “and He will repay him.” Among men it is thought a great slight when we refuse to trust them: it shows that we think them either not able to pay or not honest. What a vile affront to God it is, then, to distrust him like this! No, indeed, what horrid blasphemy, to doubt the security of what he has so expressly passed his word for, he who is the Lord of all, and therefore cannot be unable, and who is the God of truth, and therefore will not fail to make good his promise! So do not let that unbelieving fear of future want shrink and shut up your heart against your poor brother. For though he is never likely to pay, yet God becomes his surety and enters into bond with him, and will most assuredly pay you with increase.

So it is so far from being a loss to you to give in this way that it is #emph[your great advantage.] Any man would rather put his money in some sure hand, where he may both make it grow and be certain of having it when he needs it, than let it lie unprofitable by him, especially if he is in danger of thieves or other accidents by which he may well lose it. Now, alas, all that we possess is in imminent danger of being lost. There are countless accidents which may in an instant bring a rich man to beggary. Let him who doubts this only read the story of #emph[Job,] and he will find an example of it there. So what course could be more prudent for our wealth than to put it out of the reach of those accidents by lending it to God in this way, where we may be sure to find it ready at our greatest need, and that with profit and increase too? It is in this respect that the apostle compares alms to seed (2 Cor 9:10). We know it is the nature of seed that is sown to multiply and increase; and so do all our acts of mercy. They do not return to us single and bare, but bring their sheaves in with them, a most plentiful and bountiful harvest. God does not deal with our alms as we too often deal with his graces, wrapping them up in a napkin so that they never bring in any advantage to us. No, he makes us most rich returns. So we have every reason to set about this duty most cheerfully, yes, joyfully, since we have such invitations to it, as much in respect of our own #emph[interest] as of our neighbors’ needs.

So you#super[+] have the truth of the point proved by both divine and human testimonies. I should now go on to give you#super[+] some instances to confirm it further. But first I must beg you#super[+] to take these two #emph[cautions.]

1\. #emph[Do not ascribe the reward to any merit of your good works, but only to the free grace and rich mercy of God.] It is his rich mercy that we have the means to give, his free grace that we have a heart to give, and his abundant goodness that he accepts our works of charity and rewards them with all sorts of blessings, temporal, spiritual and eternal. But we cannot merit anything at his hand by giving him what is his own.

2\. #emph[Our alms-deeds are sacrifices acceptable and well-pleasing to God through Christ,] and he has bound himself by many gracious promises to reward them both here and hereafter. Yet far be it from us to perform them merely out of hope of reward. Let us rather perform them #emph[for conscience’ sake, in obedience to the command of God] (who has required them at our hands), and #emph[in testimony of our thankfulness to him] for what he has graciously bestowed on us. We are commanded to obey the magistrate not so much #emph[for fear of punishment] and #emph[hope of reward] as #emph[for conscience’ sake,] in obedience to the command of God (Rom 13:5). So we ought to give our alms not so much for the sake of the reward as out of conscience, for the Lord’s sake.

I do not deny that Christians in their well-doing may, for their encouragement, have regard to the recompense of reward, but not #emph[only] or #emph[chiefly.] The chief and principal ends we should aim at in all our good works are #emph[the glory of God] and #emph[obedience to his command.] Our regard for the recompense of reward ought to be subordinate to these. The truth is, this temporal reward should be looked on not so much as an #emph[argument] to persuade us to give, but as an answer to an objection against giving. You will be ready to say, I may need it myself, or those who are mine may need it. But only believe that God will repay you, and with overplus, and then that doubt will be taken out of the way.
